<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">War and march · Sacred · c. 1100 BCE · Ancient Near East</p><h1 class="sg-title">Song of Deborah</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="hbo" dir="rtl">שירת דבורה</bdi> / <bdi lang="hbo-Latn">Shirat Devorah</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A victory song in the Bible, sung by Deborah, a prophetess and judge of Israel. It is among the oldest poems in Hebrew.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>anonymous; ascribed in the Book of Judges to Deborah and Barak</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the people of ancient Israel; Jewish and Christian worshippers</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Biblical Hebrew</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>ancient Israel (not known exactly)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>proposed dates range from the 12th century BCE to the 3rd century BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>War and march · Sacred</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words anonymous (Judges 5))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>According to the Book of Judges, Deborah was a prophetess, the fourth Judge of Israel before the monarchy and the only female judge named in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>; she gave her judgments beneath a date palm between Ramah in Benjamin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethel">Bethel</a> in Ephraim. The people of Israel had been oppressed for twenty years by Jabin, the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>, whose capital was Hazor. Deborah summoned Barak, son of Abinoam, from Kedesh in Naphtali and told him that God commanded him to muster ten thousand men of Naphtali and Zebulun on Mount Tabor. Barak would not go without her; she agreed, but declared that the glory of the victory would then belong to a woman. Sisera, the commander of Jabin's army, gathered nine hundred iron <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a> and was utterly defeated; he fled on foot to the tent of Jael, who killed him while he slept. Judges 4 tells this story in prose, and the account ends with forty years of peace in the land.</p><p>Judges 5 tells the same story in poetry. This passage, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-of-deborah">Song of Deborah</a>, is a victory hymn sung by Deborah and Barak, and it differs a little from the prose account. It names six tribes that took part, Ephraim, Benjamin, Machir (a group associated with the tribe of Manasseh), Zebulun, Issachar and Naphtali, where Judges 4 has only Naphtali and Zebulun; it does not mention Jabin; it rebukes three other tribes, Reuben, Dan and Asher, for staying away, and does not mention Gad, Simeon or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>. The biblical scholar Michael Coogan writes that for the song's redactors, the death of the Canaanite general at the hands of a woman &quot;is a further sign that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yhwh">Yahweh</a> ultimately is responsible for the victory&quot;. Victory hymns are a convention of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, but this one is unusual in celebrating the victory of two women, Deborah the prophetess and Jael the warrior; Jael has been compared with the heroine of the Book of Judith.</p><p>The song is commonly counted among the oldest texts of the Bible, and with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-of-the-sea">Song of the Sea</a> in Exodus it is sometimes cited as the earliest example of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> poetry. Its date is disputed: many scholars place it as early as the 12th century BCE, others between about 700 and 450 BCE, and some as late as the 3rd century BCE. The rabbinic chronology Seder Olam Rabbah places Deborah's forty years as judge from 1107 to 1067 BC. Reading the archaeology, scholars have set the war with Sisera in the second half of the 12th century BC or of the 11th. Sisera is not a Semitic name and the story is set &quot;in the days of Shamgar&quot;, a hero known for fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philistines">Philistines</a>, so some, among them Łukasz Niesiołowski-Spanò, believe it is really about the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-peoples">Sea Peoples</a>; a layer of Hazor from about 1200 BCE shows signs of a catastrophic fire. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israel-finkelstein">Israel Finkelstein</a> placed the song's background in the early 10th century BCE, linking it with the destruction of Megiddo around 1000–985 BCE, while others find that it fits the 13th century BCE, the age of the literature of Ugarit.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A hymn of victory that gives the triumph to the God of Israel. It recalls the hard days before the rising, praises the tribes that came to fight and shames those that stayed at home, and tells how the Canaanite army was routed and its general killed by Jael. The mood is exultant and fierce, and its heroes are two women.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks sg-blocks-2"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Biblical Hebrew</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">וַתָּ֣שַׁר דְּבוֹרָ֔ה וּבָרָ֖ק בֶּן־אֲבִינֹ֑עַם בַּיּ֥וֹם הַה֖וּא לֵאמֹֽר׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">בִּפְרֹ֤עַ פְּרָעוֹת֙ בְּיִשְׂרָאֵ֔ל בְּהִתְנַדֵּ֖ב עָ֑ם בָּֽרְﬞכ֖וּ יְהֹוָֽה׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">שִׁמְע֣וּ מְלָכִ֔ים הַאֲזִ֖ינוּ רֹֽזְנִ֑ים אָנֹכִ֗י לַֽיהֹוָה֙ אָנֹכִ֣י אָשִׁ֔ירָה אֲזַמֵּ֕ר לַֽיהֹוָ֖ה אֱלֹהֵ֥י יִשְׂרָאֵֽל׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">יְהֹוָ֗ה בְּצֵאתְךָ֤ מִשֵּׂעִיר֙ בְּצַעְדְּךָ֙ מִשְּׂדֵ֣ה אֱד֔וֹם אֶ֣רֶץ רָעָ֔שָׁה גַּם־שָׁמַ֖יִם נָטָ֑פוּ גַּם־עָבִ֖ים נָ֥טְפוּ מָֽיִם׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">הָרִ֥ים נָזְל֖וּ מִפְּנֵ֣י יְהֹוָ֑ה זֶ֣ה סִינַ֔י מִפְּנֵ֕י יְהֹוָ֖ה אֱלֹהֵ֥י יִשְׂרָאֵֽל׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">בִּימֵ֞י שַׁמְגַּ֤ר בֶּן־עֲנָת֙ בִּימֵ֣י יָעֵ֔ל חָדְל֖וּ אֳרָח֑וֹת וְהֹלְכֵ֣י נְתִיב֔וֹת יֵלְכ֕וּ אֳרָח֖וֹת עֲקַלְקַלּֽוֹת׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">חָדְל֧וּ פְרָז֛וֹן בְּיִשְׂרָאֵ֖ל חָדֵ֑לּוּ עַ֤ד שַׁקַּ֙מְתִּי֙ דְּבוֹרָ֔ה שַׁקַּ֥מְתִּי אֵ֖ם בְּיִשְׂרָאֵֽל׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">יִבְחַר֙ אֱלֹהִ֣ים חֲדָשִׁ֔ים אָ֖ז לָחֶ֣ם שְׁעָרִ֑ים מָגֵ֤ן אִם־יֵֽרָאֶה֙ וָרֹ֔מַח בְּאַרְבָּעִ֥ים אֶ֖לֶף בְּיִשְׂרָאֵֽל׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">לִבִּי֙ לְחוֹקְﬞקֵ֣י יִשְׂרָאֵ֔ל הַמִּֽתְנַדְּבִ֖ים בָּעָ֑ם בָּרְﬞכ֖וּ יְהֹוָֽה׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">רֹכְבֵי֩ אֲתֹנ֨וֹת צְחֹר֜וֹת יֹשְׁבֵ֧י עַל־מִדִּ֛ין וְהֹלְכֵ֥י עַל־דֶּ֖רֶךְ שִֽׂיחוּ׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">מִקּ֣וֹל מְחַֽצְﬞצִ֗ים בֵּ֚ין מַשְׁאַבִּ֔ים שָׁ֤ם יְתַנּוּ֙ צִדְק֣וֹת יְהֹוָ֔ה צִדְקֹ֥ת פִּרְזוֹנ֖וֹ בְּיִשְׂרָאֵ֑ל אָ֛ז יָרְד֥וּ לַשְּׁעָרִ֖ים עַם־יְהֹוָֽה׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">יב עוּרִ֤י עוּרִי֙ דְּבוֹרָ֔ה ע֥וּרִי ע֖וּרִי דַּבְּרִי־שִׁ֑יר ק֥וּם בָּרָ֛ק וּֽשְׁﬞבֵ֥ה שֶׁבְיְךָ֖ בֶּן־אֲבִינֹֽעַם׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">אָ֚ז יְרַ֣ד שָׂרִ֔יד לְאַדִּירִ֖ים עָ֑ם יְהֹוָ֕ה יְרַד־לִ֖י בַּגִּבּוֹרִֽים׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">מִנִּ֣י אֶפְרַ֗יִם שׇׁרְשָׁם֙ בַּעֲמָלֵ֔ק אַחֲרֶ֥יךָ בִנְיָמִ֖ין בַּעֲמָמֶ֑יךָ מִנִּ֣י מָכִ֗יר יָֽרְדוּ֙ מְחֹ֣קְﬞקִ֔ים וּמִ֨זְּבוּלֻ֔ן מֹשְׁכִ֖ים בְּשֵׁ֥בֶט סֹפֵֽר׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">וְשָׂרַ֤י בְּיִשָּׂשכָר֙ עִם־דְּבֹרָ֔ה וְיִשָּׂשכָר֙ כֵּ֣ן בָּרָ֔ק בָּעֵ֖מֶק שֻׁלַּ֣ח בְּרַגְלָ֑יו בִּפְלַגּ֣וֹת רְאוּבֵ֔ן גְּדֹלִ֖ים חִקְקֵי־לֵֽב׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">לָ֣מָּה יָשַׁ֗בְתָּ בֵּ֚ין הַֽמִּשְׁפְּתַ֔יִם לִשְׁמֹ֖עַ שְׁרִק֣וֹת עֲדָרִ֑ים לִפְלַגּ֣וֹת רְאוּבֵ֔ן גְּדוֹלִ֖ים חִקְרֵי־לֵֽב׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">גִּלְעָ֗ד בְּעֵ֤בֶר הַיַּרְדֵּן֙ שָׁכֵ֔ן וְדָ֕ן לָ֥מָּה יָג֖וּר אֳנִיּ֑וֹת אָשֵׁ֗ר יָשַׁב֙ לְח֣וֹף יַמִּ֔ים וְעַ֥ל מִפְרָצָ֖יו יִשְׁכּֽוֹן׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">זְבֻל֗וּן עַ֣ם חֵרֵ֥ף נַפְשׁ֛וֹ לָמ֖וּת וְנַפְתָּלִ֑י עַ֖ל מְרוֹמֵ֥י שָׂדֶֽה׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">בָּ֤אוּ מְלָכִים֙ נִלְחָ֔מוּ אָ֤ז נִלְחֲמוּ֙ מַלְכֵ֣י כְנַ֔עַן בְּתַעְנַ֖ךְ עַל־מֵ֣י מְגִדּ֑וֹ בֶּ֥צַע כֶּ֖סֶף לֹ֥א לָקָֽחוּ׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">מִן־שָׁמַ֖יִם נִלְחָ֑מוּ הַכּֽוֹכָבִים֙ מִמְּסִלּוֹתָ֔ם נִלְחֲמ֖וּ עִם־סִֽיסְרָֽא׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">נַ֤חַל קִישׁוֹן֙ גְּרָפָ֔ם נַ֥חַל קְדוּמִ֖ים נַ֣חַל קִישׁ֑וֹן תִּדְרְכִ֥י נַפְשִׁ֖י עֹֽז׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">אָ֥ז הָלְמ֖וּ עִקְּבֵי־ס֑וּס מִֽדַּהֲר֖וֹת דַּהֲר֥וֹת אַבִּירָֽיו׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">א֣וֹרוּ מֵר֗וֹז אָמַר֙ מַלְאַ֣ךְ יְהֹוָ֔ה אֹ֥רוּ אָר֖וֹר יֹשְׁבֶ֑יהָ כִּ֤י לֹֽא־בָ֙אוּ֙ לְעֶזְרַ֣ת יְהֹוָ֔ה לְעֶזְרַ֥ת יְהֹוָ֖ה בַּגִּבּוֹרִֽים׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">תְּבֹרַךְ֙ מִנָּשִׁ֔ים יָעֵ֕ל אֵ֖שֶׁת חֶ֣בֶר הַקֵּינִ֑י מִנָּשִׁ֥ים בָּאֹ֖הֶל תְּבֹרָֽךְ׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">מַ֥יִם שָׁאַ֖ל חָלָ֣ב נָתָ֑נָה בְּסֵ֥פֶל אַדִּירִ֖ים הִקְרִ֥יבָה חֶמְאָֽה׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">יָדָהּ֙ לַיָּתֵ֣ד תִּשְׁלַ֔חְנָה וִימִינָ֖הּ לְהַלְמ֣וּת עֲמֵלִ֑ים וְהָלְמָ֤ה סִֽיסְרָא֙ מָחֲקָ֣ה רֹאשׁ֔וֹ וּמָחֲצָ֥ה וְחָלְפָ֖ה רַקָּתֽוֹ׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">בֵּ֣ין רַגְלֶ֔יהָ כָּרַ֥ע נָפַ֖ל שָׁכָ֑ב בֵּ֤ין רַגְלֶ֙יהָ֙ כָּרַ֣ע נָפָ֔ל בַּאֲשֶׁ֣ר כָּרַ֔ע שָׁ֖ם נָפַ֥ל שָׁדֽוּד׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">בְּעַד֩ הַחַלּ֨וֹן נִשְׁקְפָ֧ה וַתְּיַבֵּ֛ב אֵ֥ם סִֽיסְרָ֖א בְּעַ֣ד הָאֶשְׁנָ֑ב מַדּ֗וּעַ בֹּשֵׁ֤שׁ רִכְבּוֹ֙ לָב֔וֹא מַדּ֣וּעַ אֶֽחֱר֔וּ פַּעֲמֵ֖י מַרְכְּבוֹתָֽיו׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">חַכְמ֥וֹת שָׂרוֹתֶ֖יהָ תַּעֲנֶ֑ינָּה אַף־הִ֕יא תָּשִׁ֥יב אֲמָרֶ֖יהָ לָֽהּ׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">הֲלֹ֨א יִמְצְא֜וּ יְחַלְּק֣וּ שָׁלָ֗ל רַ֤חַם רַחֲמָתַ֙יִם֙ לְרֹ֣אשׁ גֶּ֔בֶר שְׁלַ֤ל צְבָעִים֙ לְסִ֣יסְרָ֔א שְׁלַ֥ל צְבָעִ֖ים רִקְמָ֑ה צֶ֥בַע רִקְמָתַ֖יִם לְצַוְּארֵ֥י שָׁלָֽל׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">כֵּ֠ן יֹאבְד֤וּ כׇל־אוֹיְבֶ֙יךָ֙ יְהֹוָ֔ה וְאֹ֣הֲבָ֔יו כְּצֵ֥את הַשֶּׁ֖מֶשׁ בִּגְבֻרָת֑וֹ וַתִּשְׁקֹ֥ט הָאָ֖רֶץ אַרְבָּעִ֥ים שָׁנָֽה׃</span></p></div></div><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Then sang Deborah and Barak the son of Abinoam on that day, saying,</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Praise ye the LORD for the avenging of Israel, when the people willingly offered themselves.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Hear, O ye kings; give ear, O ye princes; I, even I, will sing unto the LORD; I will sing praise to the LORD God of Israel.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>LORD, when thou wentest out of Seir, when thou marchedst out of the field of Edom, the earth trembled, and the heavens dropped, the clouds also dropped water.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The mountains melted from before the LORD, even that Sinai from before the LORD God of Israel.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>In the days of Shamgar the son of Anath, in the days of Jael, the highways were unoccupied, and the travellers walked through byways.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The inhabitants of the villages ceased, they ceased in Israel, until that I Deborah arose, that I arose a mother in Israel.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>They chose new gods; then was war in the gates: was there a shield or spear seen among forty thousand in Israel?</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>My heart is toward the governors of Israel, that offered themselves willingly among the people. Bless ye the LORD.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Speak, ye that ride on white asses, ye that sit in judgment, and walk by the way.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>They that are delivered from the noise of archers in the places of drawing water, there shall they rehearse the righteous acts of the LORD, even the righteous acts toward the inhabitants of his villages in Israel: then shall the people of the LORD go down to the gates.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Awake, awake, Deborah: awake, awake, utter a song: arise, Barak, and lead thy captivity captive, thou son of Abinoam.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Then he made him that remaineth have dominion over the nobles among the people: the LORD made me have dominion over the mighty.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Out of Ephraim was there a root of them against Amalek; after thee, Benjamin, among thy people; out of Machir came down governors, and out of Zebulun they that handle the pen of the writer.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>And the princes of Issachar were with Deborah; even Issachar, and also Barak: he was sent on foot into the valley. For the divisions of Reuben there were great thoughts of heart.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Why abodest thou among the sheepfolds, to hear the bleatings of the flocks? For the divisions of Reuben there were great searchings of heart.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Gilead abode beyond Jordan: and why did Dan remain in ships? Asher continued on the sea shore, and abode in his breaches.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Zebulun and Naphtali were a people that jeoparded their lives unto the death in the high places of the field.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The kings came and fought, then fought the kings of Canaan in Taanach by the waters of Megiddo; they took no gain of money.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>They fought from heaven; the stars in their courses fought against Sisera.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The river of Kishon swept them away, that ancient river, the river Kishon. O my soul, thou hast trodden down strength.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Then were the horsehoofs broken by the means of the pransings, the pransings of their mighty ones.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Curse ye Meroz, said the angel of the LORD, curse ye bitterly the inhabitants thereof; because they came not to the help of the LORD, to the help of the LORD against the mighty.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Blessed above women shall Jael the wife of Heber the Kenite be, blessed shall she be above women in the tent.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>He asked water, and she gave him milk; she brought forth butter in a lordly dish.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>She put her hand to the nail, and her right hand to the workmen's hammer; and with the hammer she smote Sisera, she smote off his head, when she had pierced and stricken through his temples.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>At her feet he bowed, he fell, he lay down: at her feet he bowed, he fell: where he bowed, there he fell down dead.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The mother of Sisera looked out at a window, and cried through the lattice, Why is his chariot so long in coming? why tarry the wheels of his chariots?</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Her wise ladies answered her, yea, she returned answer to herself,</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Have they not sped? have they not divided the prey; to every man a damsel or two; to Sisera a prey of divers colours, a prey of divers colours of needlework, of divers colours of needlework on both sides, meet for the necks of them that take the spoil?</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>So let all thine enemies perish, O LORD: but let them that love him be as the sun when he goeth forth in his might. And the land had rest forty years.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Judges 5, Hebrew Bible (Masoretic text). Text from <a href="https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%A9%D7%95%D7%A4%D7%98%D7%99%D7%9D_%D7%94" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>English: King James Version (1611). Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Bible_(King_James)%2FJudges%2FChapter_5" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
